v3.0.0 - 2026-09-02

  33,474 Total lines  -  21,133 Code lines

Main changes:
  -> Comment lines are now counted and reported separately, with their own percentage.
  -> Module support: a target can now be given a name, and the report is grouped by it
      as well as by language. E.g.: "mezura frontend=./web backend=./api"
  -> A project can now carry its own settings, in a ".mezura" folder, holding a
      "config.txt" and a log of its own. A run looks for it in the directory holding its targets and
      then in each directory above it, so it applies from anywhere inside the project.
      Save a config file locally in your project with the new "--save-local" flag. The targets
      are saved relative to the project so the folder can be shared.
  -> Every piece of printed text can now be styled, not just the four colors of the overview.
      More than 70 tokens are exposed, and each can take a foreground color, background color
      plus bold, italic, underline, dim, and reverse styling. They apply in order: built-in
      defaults, then the theme, then your configuration file, then "--style".
  -> Animation support for long running blocking operations with real time progress bars and spinners.
      The live lines follow the terminal's width while it is resized, and erase themselves before
      the report prints. "--hide animations" stops every moving line while keeping what they
      settle into, which a TERM=dumb terminal also gets on its own.
  -> The program can now ignore minified files and auto-generated files. Change this with
      --count-minified and --count-generated.
  -> New "MEZURA_DATA_DIR" environment variable, naming the folder mezura extracts its
      data files.
  -> MCP support, with the mezura-mcp bin target.
      A coding assistant can now run mezura for you. It comes in the release archive beside mezura,
      or with "cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/subamanis/mezura mezura-mcp".
  -> 51 new languages, bringing the shipped set to 81: Objective-C, MATLAB, Lua, Perl,
      Prolog, Pascal, Delphi, OCaml, F#, Elm, Clojure, Lisp, Scheme, Julia, D, Carbon, COBOL,
      Fortran, Ada, VHDL, Erlang, GraphQL, PowerShell, Svelte, JSX, WebAssembly text, Assembly,
      Astro, Vim Script, Processing, Emacs Lisp,
      GDScript, Verilog, SystemVerilog, Nix, HCL (Terraform), Groovy, Protocol Buffers, Visual
      Basic, Less, Solidity, Batch, Make, Dockerfile, CMake, and the shader languages HLSL, GLSL,
      WGSL, Metal, Slang and ShaderLab.
  -> More extensions are recognised for the languages already shipped.
  -> A file of a contested extension is identified by its content: a "#!" line first, then
      evidence like a line starting with "@interface" (Objective-C) or "function" (MATLAB), so one
      folder of ".m" or ".h" files splits correctly between the languages sharing it. A file whose
      content says nothing falls back to "language_conflicts.txt", "--explain" names the evidence
      that decided, and the new "--no-heuristics" turns the reading off.
  -> Non-code files wearing a language's extension, like ".d" dependency files and ProGuard ".pro"
      configurations, are now skipped and reported. "--count-not-code" counts them anyway.
  -> The new "--show-skipped" names every file left out as minified, generated or not code, and
      "--explain" says when the file it explains would be left out of a directory scan.
  -> "--force-language", "--languages" and "--exclude-languages" can now be specified per-module.
      Write the module and a slash before the value: "mezura ios=./ios analysis=./matlab
      --force-language ios/m=objective-c,analysis/m=matlab" counts one repository's ".m" files as
      Objective-C in one folder and as MATLAB in the other, in a single run.
  -> A ".ignore" or ".rgignore" is now obeyed alongside the ".gitignore".
  -> Shebang support added. A file with no extension and a name nothing claims is now opened
      for its first line, and a "#!" there decides its language. A language file declares its
      interpreters under a new optional "Shebangs" block.
  -> Language files are far more expressive, so a language can be described more accurately.
      New blocks declare the escape character, strings that cross lines apart
      from ones that end with them, raw strings, string openers and closers written differently,
      character literals, line continuations and what they carry over, more than one block comment
      pair, comment pairs that nest, and Lua's long brackets at any level. Any number of each may
      be declared, where only the first one or two used to be read.
  -> Files known by their whole name are now counted: Makefile, Dockerfile, CMakeLists.txt,
      Gemfile, Rakefile and the rest. A language file names them in a new "Filenames" field, and a
      contest over a name is settled in "language_conflicts.txt" under "contested-filenames".
  -> A language can now be named by any extension it claims, wherever a language name is expected:
      "--languages js", "--exclude-languages rs", and the same inside a configuration file.
  -> The script and style sections of HTML, Vue, Svelte and Astro files are now counted as nested
      languages
  -> Language files are now updated automatically when you install a new version, so a correction
      to a language reaches you without you having to do anything. One you had changed yourself is
      replaced too, but your copy is kept under "data/replaced/<version>/<date and time>/" and the
      program names it. A language file of your own is never touched, and neither are your themes,
      your default configuration or "language_conflicts.txt".
  -> Two languages that claim the same extension no longer settle it silently. The winner is named
      in the new "language_conflicts.txt" file of the data directory, and when nothing names it the
      program says which language it gave the extension to and that it only did so alphabetically.
  -> When a file's content names neither side, MATLAB takes ".m" and Perl takes ".pl", while ".h"
      stays with C Header. If those defaults read your files wrongly, swap the line for it in
      "language_conflicts.txt", or run with "--force-language m=objective-c".
  -> Pascal no longer claims ".pp", since almost every ".pp" file in the wild is a Puppet manifest.
      It keeps ".pas" and ".lpr".
  -> Extensions are now matched without regard to case, so a file named "MAIN.RS" counts as Rust.

(Breaking!)
  -> Color palettes are now called themes, and carry a whole look instead of four bar colors.
      "--color-palette" is now "--theme", "--show-palettes" is "--show-themes", "--tune-palettes"
      is "--theme-editor", and the files live in "data/themes" instead of "data/palettes". A theme
      file is a list of "token = value" lines, the same tokens "--style" takes, so palettes from
      older versions have to be rewritten.
  -> --colors is removed. The colors of the overview are now five ordinary style tokens,
      "language-1" to "language-4" and "language-others", so they are set like every other style.
      A configuration file can no longer declare "colors".
  -> A configuration file can no longer turn on logging: a hand-added "log" line is ignored and
      named when the file loads. Ask for an entry per run, with "--log".
  -> --no-visual and --no-keywords are removed, replaced by --hide. Use "--hide bar" and
      "--hide keywords".
  -> The default order of the languages is now the number of lines, ties broken alphabetically.
      Use "--sort size" to get the old order back.
  -> The "details" section is now a table by default, one aligned row per language. Use
      "--layout list" for an approximation of the layout that older versions printed.
  -> The section that compares this run with previous ones is now called "History" instead of
      "Progress". "Progress" now means the live bar of the run itself, and nothing else.
  -> A command given twice in one command line now stops the run, instead of the second value
      quietly replacing the first. A section declared twice in a configuration file is pointed
      out instead.
  -> "extra" no longer includes the comments: it is the blank lines plus the lines that carry
      no content.
  -> Lines that carry no content are recognised consistently now, whatever punctuation they are
      made of, so a small number of lines move between "code" and "extra".
  -> A comment line carrying no words now counts as extra, the way a lone brace always did: the
      bare "/*" and "*/" of a block written on their own lines, a banner's "*", a lone "//" spacer,
      a decorative "/*----*/". Comment counts drop visibly on trees written in banner style; code
      counts do not move.
  -> The log is now written as JSON, one entry per line, in "data/logs/<config>.jsonl". Logs from
      earlier versions (.txt) are not read or touched; delete them if you do not want them around.
  -> Warnings and errors are written to the error output instead of the standard one, so that a
      redirected or piped run carries the report and nothing else. On screen nothing changes.
  -> mezura exits with code 1 when the run failed: an unrecognised command, a wrong value for one,
      a configuration, theme or language name that does not exist, a path that is not there, or a
      set of files where every single one failed to be parsed. Finding no code to count is not a
      failure and still exits 0, and so does a run where only some files failed.

New commands:
  -> --counting content|region : switches the way mezura groups the lines. "content" is the
      default and judges a line by what it holds; "region" judges it by the span it sits in, which
      is how most other counters work.
  -> --hide <list> : leaves parts of the output unprinted. Takes any of version, directory-info,
      parsing-info, progress-bar, animations, keywords, overview, bar, nested-languages, history
      and timing, separated by commas or spaces. It also takes out columns of the details:
      "files", "comments", "extra", "size" and "percentages". Errors and warnings are never hidden.
  -> --style <token>=<value>,... : overrides the style of one or more categories of printed text,
      for a single run. Can also be stored in a configuration file and is kept by --save.
  -> --explain : run on exactly one file, it answers how the counter classifies each line, one by
      one. It also takes a range of lines, "--explain 1210..1230", with either end free to be left
      off.
  -> --by-file [n] : every counted file gets a row of its own under the language it was counted as.
      A number keeps only the n biggest of each language, by whatever "--sort" is in effect.
  -> --force-language <ext>=<language>,... : decides which language an extension or a whole file
      name belongs to for this run, whether or not another language claims it. It overrides the
      conflicts file and can be saved in a configuration file.
  -> --save-local : writes the settings of this run into the project's own ".mezura" folder, with
      the targets relative to the project. "--no-local" ignores that folder for one run.
  -> --bar-thickness slim|medium|fat|low : chooses the character the overview bar is drawn with.
      "slim" is the only ASCII one, so it is the fallback for terminals that cannot render box
      drawing characters.
  -> --progress-bar smooth|blocky|hash : chooses the characters the live bar is drawn with. "hash"
      is plain ASCII, for a terminal that draws the block characters wrongly or not at all.
  -> --layout table|boxed|list|matrix : chooses the shape of the "details" section. "boxed" is the
      table inside a drawn frame, "list" is one row per language with the size after a "|", the
      shape older versions printed, and "matrix" puts the languages down and the modules across,
      one number per cell.
  -> --output text|json : "json" replaces the whole printed report with one JSON document, so a run
      can be read by another program. Counts go in as plain numbers, with no separators and no
      percentages, and warnings are carried under "warnings", each with a code, a subject, a
      readable message and whether it puts the counts or only the settings in doubt. The document
      is written even when there was nothing to count and even when every file failed to parse.
  -> --diff <reading> or <older>..<newer> : compares this run against another reading, or two
      readings against each other. A reading is a JSON document an earlier run wrote, or a git
      revision: "mezura ./src --diff main" counts what "./src" held on "main", and
      "--diff v2.0.1..v3.0.0" compares two tags. Every figure carries what it is now and how much
      it moved, and a language only one side has is marked "new" or "gone". The settings recorded
      in a document reach whatever is counted against it, so the two sides measure the same thing.
  -> --save-theme <name> : writes the way this run looks into a theme file, flattened into values,
      so it stands on its own and can be shared.
  -> --number-separator comma|underscore|dot|none : chooses the character that groups the digits of
      every printed number. The character itself is accepted too, as in "--number-separator _".
  -> --decimal-separator dot|comma : chooses the character that separates the decimals of the sizes,
      the percentages and the execution time. Together with the one above, "1.559.486" and "716,2".
  -> --sort lines|files|code|comments|extra|size|name : chooses the order of the languages.
  -> --version : prints the version of the binary and the date it was released on.
  -> --restore : writes back every file that ships with mezura and is not in your data directory
      any more, and says which ones. Your own files and your edits to the shipped ones are never
      touched.
  -> --top <n> : shows only the first n languages, and states underneath how many were hidden.
      The total keeps counting all of them, and the overview never shows more than n either.

Change in commands:
  -> The "--dirs" command is now "--targets", and a saved configuration's "===> dirs" block is now
      "===> targets". Edit or re-save old configurations, or their targets are ignored.
  -> "--braces-as-code" is removed: replace a configuration's "===> braces-as-code" block with a
      "===> counting" block holding "region" to keep that behaviour.
  -> "--show-themes" previews a theme on a sample of real details rows and a mock overview, instead
      of the overview alone. It also takes an optional thickness, "--show-themes fat".
  -> The theme editor page opened by "--theme-editor" now shows a whole run, details table and
      overview, and gives a picker to each of the 29 tokens that draw them. Three tokens of the
      overview could not be changed there at all before, and a theme that dresses more than the
      overview now reaches the page whole, backgrounds and bold and italics included.
  -> The history section marks an entry that was counted with different settings: "modified:"
      followed by the names of the ones that changed. Log entries also record "--no-gitignore" now.
  -> The "Exec time" figure now measures the whole program time instead of only the scan.

Performance:
  -> Counting uses well under half the processor time it did, while counting more languages than
      before, and the counts themselves are unchanged. How much of that reaches the clock is decided
      by your disk: a large repository on a fast NVMe was already close to what the processor can
      do.
  -> mezura now uses many more threads for reading files, because a thread spends most of its time
      waiting for a file to open rather than working. This is worth nothing on a fast disk whose
      files were read recently, and up to twice the speed on a slow disk or on the first run after
      a reboot. "--threads" accepts up to 32 producers and 128 consumers now, instead of 8 and 30,
      so it is worth raising if your files live on a slow drive.

Bugfixes:
  -> A setting your default configuration holds is no longer dropped when you load another one.
      "targets", "exclude-languages", "top" and "by-file" were the four that could go missing.
  -> A block comment that closes and reopens on the same line no longer counts the rest of the file
      as code. This hit Lua and HTML, whose opening and closing symbols differ in length.
  -> HTML declares no string symbols at all now: its quotes delimit attributes, not strings, so an
      apostrophe in the text ("it's") no longer swallows the comments and markup below it.
  -> A log is never emptied by a run that could not read it. It used to be cleared and rebuilt from
      what had just been read, so a file that was locked or damaged came back holding that one run
      where it had held every previous one, without a word.
  -> A log you emptied by hand is written to again.
  -> A language file with a mistyped heading is now reported as a formatting problem instead of
      being read half way.
  -> A language file that could not be read at all is no longer reported as a formatting problem.
      A file saved in an encoding mezura cannot read now says so, and one whose format really is
      wrong names the line the reading stopped at.
  -> Two language files whose names differ only in upper and lower case are now reported, and
      "--force-language" and "language_conflicts.txt" give the extension to the one you named. Both
      used to fold the case away and pick the other one, so naming a language exactly as it is
      spelled could hand your files to a different one, with its comment symbols and without a word.
  -> Two language files that declare the same language name are now reported. One of them was used
      and the other discarded in silence, and which one won depended on the file names, so renaming
      a file could change your counts.
  -> A language that claims one extension in two spellings, such as "h" and ".h", no longer reports
      itself as contesting the extension with itself.
  -> An extension can now be written with the dot in front of it. ".rs" in a language file, and
      ".m" in "language_conflicts.txt", used to match nothing at all and say nothing about it.
  -> "--exclude-languages" no longer says that a language does not exist when it does, and does say
      so when it is given a name that really is not one, instead of silently excluding nothing.
  -> The "N files found" and "Parsing files" lines now appear the moment the directories have been
      walked, while the files are still being counted, instead of arriving with the results.
  -> A target whose path exists is now always taken literally: a directory with "[" or "{" in its
      name, or a run started inside one, used to be refused as an invalid glob pattern.
  -> The very first run after a fresh install now counts keywords (classes, structs and the rest).
      They were reported as zero until the second run.
  -> Naming the same directory twice, once with a slash at the end, counted everything in it twice.
      "mezura ./src,./src/" now reports what "mezura ./src" reports.
  -> A directory that cannot be opened during the scan is now reported, instead of taking everything
      inside it out of the counts without a word.
  -> The paths of faulty files are now written with forward slashes throughout, instead of mixing
      them with backslashes halfway through on Windows.
  -> A scan that finds nothing after failing to open a directory now exits with a failure code,
      the same way a run where every file failed to parse always has: that zero is not a count of
      anything. Finding nothing in directories that could actually be read still exits 0.
  -> A path with "--" inside it, like the folder names some tools generate, is no longer cut at it
      and refused. A command is also only recognised as a whole word now, so "--helpme" is an
      unrecognised command instead of the help.
  -> A configuration file that stops being readable partway, most often because it was saved in an
      encoding other than UTF-8, now stops the run and names the file and the line. It used to be
      applied up to the unreadable line and silently lose everything after it.
  -> A worker thread dying mid-run now stops the run with an error that carries the reason,
      instead of returning counts that are silently missing that thread's share, or in one
      arrangement never returning at all.
  -> A configuration file's "no-gitignore" and "search-in-dotted" now apply when the targets are
      being worked out, not only during the counting. A config that declared "no-gitignore" beside
      a glob of gitignored paths died with "doesn't exist anymore" while the same command line
      counted.
  -> CSS, HTML and SCSS are counted at all now. A language file that declares multiline comments
      but no keywords was rejected as badly formatted, so those three were dropped from every run
      with a one line warning and nothing else. Expect them to appear where they were missing.
  -> The shipped language files describe their languages better. Python declares triple quotes, so
      a docstring containing a single quote no longer swallows the lines after it. JavaScript,
      TypeScript, TSX, Vue and Go declare their backtick strings, and Java, Kotlin, Scala, Swift,
      C# and Elixir their triple quoted blocks. More extensions are recognised too: cc, cxx and c++
      for C++, hh, hxx and h++ for C++ headers, mjs, cjs and jsx for JavaScript, mts and cts for
      TypeScript, pyw and pyi for Python, zsh and ksh for Shell, csx for C#, and sc for Scala.
  -> A string that ends in a backslash no longer runs to the end of the file in Go, Odin, D,
      Kotlin, Scala, C#, F#, Shell and PowerShell. A path like `C:\` used to leave the string open,
      and everything under it, comments included, was counted as code.
  -> An unbalanced quote now costs its own line instead of every line below it.
  -> A commented-out block holding another block, and a "]]" inside a Lua "--[==[", are read to
      their real end instead of stopping at the first closer.
  -> A string that begins exactly where a block comment closes, "*/" straight into a quote with no
      space between, is now read to its real end. Its opening quote used to be taken as its closing
      one, so the string's text was counted as code, and a quote that crosses lines could carry the
      mistake to the end of the file.
  -> A string that stays open because every symbol that could close it is escaped is no longer cut
      short at a different string symbol on the same line. Languages with more than one string
      symbol, Python and Shell among them, count a few such lines differently than before.
  -> Deleting the languages or the configuration folder no longer costs you your themes and your
      default configuration. The program used to treat that as a fresh installation and write every
      shipped file over the top; now it only puts back what is missing.
  -> A data directory whose files went missing is filled again on the next run. Deleting the
      "languages" or the "logs" folder used to leave it empty until the next version of mezura,
      while the run silently counted with the copies inside the program.
  -> A run that cannot update its data files now counts with the language files inside the program
      and says so. A folder with one language file in it used to pass for a whole installation, and
      everything else went uncounted without a word.
  -> Anything of mezura's own that goes missing is now named when it is written again, instead of
      coming back in silence. A first run, which has lost nothing, still says nothing.
  -> The update message no longer says you changed a file when all it knows is that the file is not
      the one it wrote.
  -> The percentage bar of the overview is now more accurate.
  -> A theme file that cannot be fully read no longer stops the program. The lines that do not
      parse are reported and skipped, and the rest of the file still applies.
  -> A section a config file declares that is not a command is now reported instead of being
      ignored in silence.
  -> A run typed with no arguments at all now takes its targets from a configuration file that names
      them. Before, it always counted the working directory, so a "===> targets" block could only
      reach a run that typed at least one command beside it.

Dependencies:
  -> serde_json = "1", for the JSON the program now writes and reads.
  -> terminal_size = "0.4", so the live animated lines can fit themselves to the terminal's width.
  -> unicode-width = "0.2", so a name is measured in the terminal columns it really occupies.

Other:
  -> Every percentage drops decimals as it grows: two below 10, one below 100, none from 100 up.
      A change past 999% is printed as "+ >999%", the way a change under 0.01% reads "+ <0.01%".
  -> "--search-in-dotted" no longer walks into ".git". It opens the dotted directories somebody
      made, like ".github", and leaves the one git keeps alone, at any depth. Naming ".git" as a
      target yourself still works.
  -> A symbolic link, or a Windows junction, that the scan comes across is no longer followed, so
      whatever it points at is not counted a second time through it. Expect a smaller count on a
      project that links one of its own directories in. One you name as a target is still followed.
  -> A name that does not exist now says what it is close to, instead of only that it is wrong.
      This covers an unrecognised command, a theme, a configuration and the names given to
      "--languages". When nothing is close, it points at the command that lists what exists.
  -> Updated default styling. The "list" layout was rebuilt too: one row per language, reading
      left to right, with the size after a "|" at the end.
  -> Sizes are written "B", "KB", "MB" and "GB" instead of "Bytes", "KBs", "MBs" and "GBs", a whole
      number of bytes is printed without a decimal, and a gigabyte and above is reported in GB.
  -> Every message the program prints now wraps to the width of the report, breaking between words
      instead of wherever the terminal happens to end the line.
  -> Every error now names what failed and what to do about it, and one word is used for one thing
      throughout ("directory", never "dir" or "folder").
  -> "--show-languages" prints its list in three columns instead of one name per line, so the whole
      set fits on a screen.
  -> A language with many keywords wraps its keyword row, instead of making the details section
      wider than every other line in it.
  -> mezura is now dual-licensed as MIT OR Apache-2.0, at your option.
  -> The help is printed in colour.
  -> "--help" is now one line per command instead of every command in full. "--help full" prints
      all of it, and "--help --targets" one of it, as before.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



v2.0.1 - 2026-07-27

  6,320 Total lines  -  4,531 Code lines

Bugfixes:
  -> Fixed the program refusing to start on a machine where it had never been executed before, but
      where the test suite had, reporting that the language dir "has been deleted". Merely reading
      the persistent paths used to create the application folder as a side effect, so the test suite
      created an empty one, which every subsequent run then mistook for a complete installation.
      The paths are now created only during the actual initialization, and an installation counts as
      complete only when the language, config and log dirs are really there.
  -> An incomplete application folder is now completed on the next execution, instead of the program
      either failing or wiping the whole folder. A missing or emptied "languages" dir is regenerated
      in place, leaving the "config" and "logs" folders untouched, and a failure in the middle of the
      initialization no longer deletes the saved configurations and logs.
  -> Fixed two tests that only passed on a machine with an already initialized application folder,
      since they wrote to the persistent config dir without creating it.

Other:
  -> The error messages about unreadable language files now suggest deleting only the "languages"
      folder, which is regenerated automatically, instead of the whole application folder.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



v2.0.0 - 2026-07-26

  6,312 Total lines  -  4,534 Code lines

Main changes:
  -> (Breaking!) The program now respects .gitignore files by default: ignored files and folders are
      skipped, with support for nested .gitignore files and negated patterns. Explicitly given
      target dirs are always traversed, even if a .gitignore would ignore them. Expect different
      (more meaningful) stats and much faster runs on directories containing build artifacts or
      dependencies. The --no-gitignore flag restores the old behavior.

Performance:
  -> Various optimizations that yielded significant performance improvements, especially when
      analyzing large directory trees. You can expect a ~2x performance increase on hot runs,
      and much more consistent run times, since the worst cases were eliminated.
      Runs on repositories are additionally much faster now, because of the .gitignore support.

Bugfixes:
  -> Fixed nondeterministic language attribution for extensions claimed by multiple language files.
      Each run could previously assign such files to a different language, changing the reported
      stats between identical runs on the same directory. Files are now always counted under the
      alphabetically first language that claims the extension.
  -> Fixed nondeterministic ordering of keyword occurrences in the results. Keywords are now
      printed in alphabetical order, stable across runs.
  -> Fixed a race condition in the parallel directory traversal and file parsing, that could slow down
      the end of a run and, in rare cases, skip files entirely.
  -> Fixed the detection of mutually exclusive --languages and --exclude-languages combinations.
      It was previously missed when the excluded list was the larger one, resulting in a misleading
      "No relevant files found" report.
  -> Fixed the rewriting of configuration and language files, which could leave stale content at the
      end of a file when the new content was shorter, corrupting it. Errors while writing files are
      also no longer silently ignored.
  -> Fixed the default configuration file never actually being loaded (the program was looking for
      a file named "default.txt.txt"), which meant that any preferences saved in it were silently
      ignored on every run.
  -> Fixed the same files being counted twice when a target path was given more than once, or when
      a target path was contained in another one, like 'mezura src, src/utils'. Such targets are
      now dropped, keeping only the topmost one.

New commands:
  -> Added the --no-gitignore flag, that disables the new default of respecting .gitignore files.
  -> Added the --colors command, that overrides the colors of the "overview" section with
      1 to 5 custom colors (hex values or standard terminal color names).
  -> Added the --color-palette command, that applies a named color palette to the "overview"
      section. 8 palettes are bundled by default (Mezura, Dracula, Nord, Gruvbox, Catppuccin,
      Sunset, Neon, Ocean), as editable files in the new "data/palettes" folder, where custom
      palettes can also be added.
  -> Added the --show-palettes command, that displays the color palettes that were found,
      each one previewed on a mock overview with its own colors.
  -> Added the --tune-palettes command, that generates an interactive HTML page with all the
      detected color palettes and opens it in the browser, where their colors can be adjusted
      with live contrast metrics and turned into ready commands.

Change in commands:
  -> The target paths (--dirs) now support glob patterns (* ? [..] {..}), which are expanded to every
      existing directory and file that they match, so 'services/*/src' is a valid target. The matches
      of a pattern are skipped if a .gitignore ignores them or if they are dotted, exactly like every
      other path that the program discovers, while the paths that are written out explicitly are
      always used.
  -> The --exclude command now supports glob patterns (* ? [..] {..}), and applies one consistent
      rule for both files and folders: a pattern without a slash matches a name at any depth, and a
      pattern with slashes matches the end of the path, anchored at path components. Previously,
      folder patterns only matched exact names or full paths, and file patterns could silently
      match mid-name path suffixes.
  -> The --threads command now accepts up to 8 producers (instead of 4) and up to 30 consumers
      (instead of 12), and the automatically chosen numbers were tuned accordingly, since
      oversubscribing the consumers hides the latency of opening files.
  -> The --changelog command now prints only the changes of the current version.
      The full version history (most recent first) is available through '--changelog full'.

Dependencies:
  -> memchr = "2.8" was introduced, for more efficient substring matching
  -> globset = "0.4.19" and ignore = "0.4.20" were introduced, for the glob patterns of the
      --exclude command and for the .gitignore support, respectively
  -> glob = "0.3.3" was introduced, for the expansion of the glob patterns of the target paths
  -> lazy_static was removed, since the standard library now covers its functionality
  -> chrono and crossbeam-deque were updated to their latest versions, and the versions of all
      the dependencies are now pinned to the ones the program is tested with

Other:
  -> 9 more language files bundled by default: Ruby, Dart, Zig, Scala, SQL, Haskell, Shell,
      Elixir and Vue.
  -> Header files are now counted under dedicated "C Header" (.h) and "C++ Header" (.hpp) languages,
      instead of being ambiguously attributed to C or C++.
  -> Project migrated to 2024 language edition and now uses some more modern features
  -> Invalid values in configuration files are no longer silently replaced:
      the program now stops and reports exactly which value, in which config file, needs fixing,
      unless a valid command line argument overrides the faulty value (a warning is shown instead).
  -> The palette tuner is also available online, without having to install anything:
      https://subamanis.github.io/mezura/palette-tuner/



